  "client_view_text": " A process is a program in execution, which includes the program code, data, and system resources allocated to it. A thread is a flow of execution within a process, which shares the same memory space and resources as the process. In other words, a process is a container that holds one or more threads. Here are some key differences between processes and threads:\n\n1.  **Process**: A process is a separate entity that runs independently of other processes. It has its own memory space, and each process has its own copy of the program code, data, and system resources. When a process is created, a new memory space is allocated to it, and it has its own program counter, stack, and registers.\n\n2.  **Thread**: A thread is a lightweight process that shares the same memory space and resources as the process it belongs to. Threads are created within a process, and they share the same memory space, which means they can access the same data and resources. Threads have their own program counter, stack, and registers, but they share the same memory space as the process.\n\nHere are some key differences between processes and threads:\n\n*   **Memory Usage**: Processes have their own memory space, which means they require more memory to run. Threads, on the"
